WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.101 --> 00:00:02.969
- America as a nation
is founded on ideals

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:07.273
that are universal and built one

00:00:07.273 --> 00:00:10.843 align:start size:97% position:3%
of the largest freaking
economy and largest military.

00:00:10.843 --> 00:00:13.112
However, I think
that the people,

00:00:13.112 --> 00:00:15.615
those who have lived under
prosperity for a long time,

00:00:15.615 --> 00:00:17.517
and that's one of the
main contradictions,

00:00:17.517 --> 00:00:19.418 align:start size:94% position:6%
is that they start
turning against the ideas

00:00:19.418 --> 00:00:21.587 align:start size:94% position:6%
that made them
prosperous at the moment.

00:00:21.587 --> 00:00:24.390 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so one of, I
would say, the ideals

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:27.694 align:start size:84% position:16%
of Ideas Beyond
Borders is making sure

00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:30.496 align:start position:0%
that the American ideals
spread, that they can continue,

00:00:30.496 --> 00:00:32.298
kind of like when you
send humans to Mars

00:00:32.298 --> 00:00:33.967 align:start size:84% position:16%
so in that way,
humanity can continue

00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:36.336
is to send the American
ideals all over the world

00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:39.539
so in that way, these
ideals will continue.

00:00:39.539 --> 00:00:43.009
(warm instrumental music)

00:00:46.646 --> 00:00:48.348
- I'm Dave Rubin, and
we are on location

00:00:48.348 --> 00:00:50.784
here at the local
studios in Miami.

00:00:50.784 --> 00:00:55.588 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I am joined by the founder
of Ideas Beyond Borders,

00:00:55.588 --> 00:00:59.192
but more importantly
a brand-new American

00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:01.928 align:start size:84% position:16%
and my old friend,
Faisal Saeed Al Mutar.

00:01:01.928 --> 00:01:03.996
It is good to see you, my man.

00:01:03.996 --> 00:01:05.965
- Thank you so much
for having me again.

00:01:05.965 --> 00:01:07.333
It's been a journey.

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:09.836 align:start position:0%
- Faisal, we were just
discussing you, well, first off,

00:01:09.836 --> 00:01:11.370
you've been on this show.

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:14.874 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, there's the Jordan
Peterson like 15 times,

00:01:14.874 --> 00:01:17.810
there's the Gad Saad
probably 14 times,

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:20.647 align:start size:88% position:13%
the Douglas Murray
probably about 14 times.

00:01:20.647 --> 00:01:22.115 align:start size:81% position:19%
You're close.
- I'm close, yeah.

00:01:22.115 --> 00:01:23.649 align:start size:84% position:16%
- You're you're a
top fiver, for sure.

00:01:23.649 --> 00:01:26.719
- Let make it 72 so in
that way... (laughs)

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:28.554
- That was a virgin joke.
That was a virgin joke.

00:01:28.554 --> 00:01:29.756
- So that way maybe that

00:01:29.756 --> 00:01:31.691
will make me closer
to heaven, yeah.

00:01:31.691 --> 00:01:32.859
- It is...

00:01:34.193 --> 00:01:36.262 align:start size:97% position:3%
Every time I sit down with
you, it's just an absolute joy

00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:38.030
because you come from
a place in the world,

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:39.465
You come from Iraq.

00:01:39.465 --> 00:01:40.934
Well, you know what,

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:42.268 align:start size:97% position:3%
give everybody the one-minute
recap on your life.

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:43.770 align:start size:88% position:13%
Can you do it in a
minute, the life story?

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:45.071 align:start size:94% position:6%
'Cause then we'll get
into all the other stuff,

00:01:45.071 --> 00:01:46.873
but I think it sets up
such a beautiful story

00:01:46.873 --> 00:01:47.941
that you're now, what'd you say?

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:49.809
One year American?

00:01:49.809 --> 00:01:52.312 align:start size:84% position:16%
- 2019. It's been four
years, four years.

00:01:52.312 --> 00:01:53.279 align:start size:88% position:13%
- You've been a
citizen for four years.

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:55.482
- Yeah, but then COVID hit-

00:01:55.482 --> 00:01:56.916
- I've been treating you
like a brand-new citizen.

00:01:56.916 --> 00:01:58.051
- Well, let's keep it that way.

00:01:58.051 --> 00:01:59.586
- Yeah. (laughs)

00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:01.321
- Introduce me next time to
the best barbecue in town.

00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:03.088
- Yeah. (laughs)

00:02:03.088 --> 00:02:04.891
Give everybody like,
the one-minute story

00:02:04.891 --> 00:02:06.525
that gets you to America.

00:02:06.525 --> 00:02:09.596
- So I was born in Babylon
in Iraq, south of Baghdad,

00:02:09.596 --> 00:02:11.798
then under the regime
of Saddam Hussein.

00:02:11.798 --> 00:02:13.299 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I lived first
under Saddam Hussein

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:16.368 align:start position:0%
and then after the war, Al-Qaeda
took over my neighborhood.

00:02:16.368 --> 00:02:20.540 align:start size:88% position:13%
I decided to be
outspoken against those
crazies at the time.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:22.208
And then I ended
up on a death list,

00:02:22.208 --> 00:02:23.943 align:start size:84% position:16%
also losing many
members of my family.

00:02:23.943 --> 00:02:26.478
I left to Lebanon,
Lebanon to Malaysia,

00:02:26.478 --> 00:02:28.547 align:start size:97% position:3%
Malaysia to eventually be
accepted in the United States

00:02:28.547 --> 00:02:30.150
as a refugee in 2013.

00:02:31.451 --> 00:02:35.121 align:start size:97% position:3%
And for me, like, the
reason why when I moved here

00:02:35.121 --> 00:02:38.091
and seeing like, people not...

00:02:38.091 --> 00:02:40.325 align:start size:84% position:16%
I thought at the
beginning that like,

00:02:40.325 --> 00:02:41.694 align:start size:84% position:16%
people would
appreciate the values

00:02:41.694 --> 00:02:44.597
that I used to fight
for when I was in Iraq.

00:02:44.597 --> 00:02:48.668
And now 10 years later, I
think is that I was wrong.

00:02:48.668 --> 00:02:49.869
- [Dave] Oof!

00:02:49.869 --> 00:02:51.504 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Which is-
- That is a condemnation

00:02:51.504 --> 00:02:54.541 align:start size:94% position:6%
of your new country.
- It's not a condemnation

00:02:54.541 --> 00:02:56.743
of the country, I would say.

00:02:56.743 --> 00:03:01.314
And that's, again, the journey
is that I've had an amazing,

00:03:01.314 --> 00:03:03.483
pleasant experience in America.

00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:05.017
I'm so grateful to be here.

00:03:05.017 --> 00:03:08.287 align:start size:97% position:3%
And in fact, the word grateful
is even an understatement.

00:03:08.287 --> 00:03:11.324
A year after I moved in, I was
hosted by an American family

00:03:11.324 --> 00:03:12.992
for a year in Northern Virginia,

00:03:12.992 --> 00:03:15.328 align:start size:84% position:16%
in which they treated
me as their kid.

00:03:15.328 --> 00:03:18.731
The amount of support from
people like you and friends

00:03:18.731 --> 00:03:20.567 align:start size:84% position:16%
and others who like,
elevated my voice

00:03:20.567 --> 00:03:23.569
and made me part
of like, the scene.

00:03:23.569 --> 00:03:25.037
I don't think this...

00:03:25.037 --> 00:03:26.472
If I immigrate to
another country,

00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:29.374 align:start position:0%
I would not have that experience
so I'm amazingly grateful.

00:03:29.374 --> 00:03:32.412
And that's what I think is
the kind of the contradiction

00:03:32.412 --> 00:03:34.147
is that there's my experience,

00:03:34.147 --> 00:03:36.748 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I know it's the
experience of many thousands

00:03:36.748 --> 00:03:38.951 align:start size:88% position:13%
if not tens of
thousands of immigrants.

00:03:38.951 --> 00:03:41.554
And then there is those like
kind of champagne socialists

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:45.257
in Williamsburg and other
places who will be like,

00:03:45.257 --> 00:03:46.759
"Oh, this country sucks,

00:03:46.759 --> 00:03:50.096
and I cannot get my
latte at 2:00 PM."

00:03:50.096 --> 00:03:53.733
And not only that is
that this country is,

00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:55.301
they try to speak on behalf

00:03:55.301 --> 00:03:59.572
of those who claim that
they are the victims,

00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:04.076
and that pisses me
off to say the least.

00:04:04.076 --> 00:04:05.311
- To say the least, yes.

00:04:05.311 --> 00:04:07.513
- So one of the things
that I've been like,

00:04:07.513 --> 00:04:12.585 align:start size:97% position:3%
kind of thinking about is that
how, like, the people here,

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:15.955
we need to figure out a way

00:04:15.955 --> 00:04:18.824
to make them realize what
they're taking for granted.

00:04:18.824 --> 00:04:21.694
And I think is that it's not
just my voice that matters,

00:04:21.694 --> 00:04:23.396
but I think, I mean,
even with your show,

00:04:23.396 --> 00:04:24.864
you've interviewed
a lot of people.

00:04:24.864 --> 00:04:26.131
You've interviewed Yeonmi Park.

00:04:26.131 --> 00:04:27.667
You interviewed a
lot of the voices

00:04:27.667 --> 00:04:29.535
that perhaps speak
the same message

00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:31.637
because the thing is like,
we don't even have to speak.

00:04:31.637 --> 00:04:33.706
It is just true, so that's...

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:36.476
Sometimes I try not to speak
so much because I was like,

00:04:36.476 --> 00:04:38.878 align:start size:81% position:19%
it's just like
it's self-evident.

00:04:38.878 --> 00:04:40.079
- [Dave] Yeah.

00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:41.547 align:start size:84% position:16%
- As written in
the US Constitution

00:04:41.547 --> 00:04:43.983 align:start size:84% position:16%
or Declaration of
Independence, I guess.

00:04:43.983 --> 00:04:45.450 align:start size:84% position:16%
- You are an American.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:47.753 align:start size:94% position:6%
- You studied for the
test. I got it, I got it.

00:04:47.753 --> 00:04:50.523
Look, the first time we met was
the day that you did my show

00:04:50.523 --> 00:04:52.124 align:start size:88% position:13%
for the first time,
right at the beginning,

00:04:52.124 --> 00:04:55.561
so this was, I think, October
of 2015, if I'm not mistaken.

00:04:55.561 --> 00:04:58.464 align:start size:97% position:3%
And Melissa Chen, our mutual
friend, introduced us,

00:04:58.464 --> 00:04:59.932 align:start size:97% position:3%
and she said, "You know, you
should just have this guy

00:04:59.932 --> 00:05:01.333 align:start size:81% position:19%
on the show and
just talk to him."

00:05:01.333 --> 00:05:03.102
And I was like, "All right,
let's see what happens."

00:05:03.102 --> 00:05:06.072
And here I met this guy who
was roughly new to America,

00:05:06.072 --> 00:05:07.840
but I was like,
he loves freedom.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:11.077 align:start size:97% position:3%
You wanna talk about classical
liberalism and ideas,

00:05:11.077 --> 00:05:13.446 align:start size:88% position:13%
which now you've turned
into this nonprofit

00:05:13.446 --> 00:05:15.147
and all of this stuff so you,

00:05:15.147 --> 00:05:17.016
you just freaking
love this country.

00:05:17.016 --> 00:05:18.651
I mean, it's just,
it just comes through

00:05:18.651 --> 00:05:20.019
very clearly with you.

00:05:20.019 --> 00:05:21.688
- Yeah, and the thing is like,

00:05:21.688 --> 00:05:23.789 align:start size:88% position:13%
I loved this country
even before I came here,

00:05:23.789 --> 00:05:27.994 align:start size:97% position:3%
even though my experience
with America is a mixed bag.

00:05:27.994 --> 00:05:29.762 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, my first
introduction to America

00:05:29.762 --> 00:05:33.565
was a Humvee in the front of
our house with US soldiers.

00:05:33.565 --> 00:05:35.601 align:start size:94% position:6%
- What year was that?
- That was 2003 when the-

00:05:35.601 --> 00:05:37.336 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Wow.
- 'Cause I was born

00:05:37.336 --> 00:05:40.573
at the first war, like the, so
I guess my parents were busy

00:05:40.573 --> 00:05:41.807
when there was no electricity

00:05:41.807 --> 00:05:43.742
and they had sex and
I was born, but...

00:05:43.742 --> 00:05:45.478
- So you were born during
the original Gulf War?

00:05:45.478 --> 00:05:47.513 align:start size:97% position:3%
- The first Gulf War and then-
- So that was '92.

00:05:47.513 --> 00:05:50.916
- End of 1990 and then
'91 is when I was born.

00:05:50.916 --> 00:05:52.084
- Yeah.

00:05:52.084 --> 00:05:53.953 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And then the
second one is 2003,

00:05:53.953 --> 00:05:56.389
and I remember that
day very vividly

00:05:56.389 --> 00:05:58.824 align:start size:88% position:13%
is that we were watching
the local television

00:05:58.824 --> 00:06:00.626
and there was also
Iranian television

00:06:00.626 --> 00:06:01.927
was intercepting in Iraq.

00:06:01.927 --> 00:06:03.896
And they were telling
us that the US military

00:06:03.896 --> 00:06:05.431
is being defeated in the South

00:06:05.431 --> 00:06:07.132
and there are all these
soldiers being killed

00:06:07.132 --> 00:06:09.301 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the Iraqis are winning
and stuff like that.

00:06:09.301 --> 00:06:13.005 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then my brother was in the
second floor and he's like,

00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:16.142
"There are American tanks
in front of our house."

00:06:17.543 --> 00:06:21.480
And then within like, half an
hour, like the American tanks

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:23.783 align:start size:81% position:19%
were bombing my
elementary school,

00:06:23.783 --> 00:06:26.585
which has a military
base of the Iraqi Army.

00:06:26.585 --> 00:06:31.657 align:start position:0%
And within like, a couple days,
so that was in early April,

00:06:32.457 --> 00:06:33.859
pretty much all of the highway,

00:06:33.859 --> 00:06:36.295
which our house was in
front of the highway

00:06:36.295 --> 00:06:37.897
where the US military
came into Baghdad.

00:06:37.897 --> 00:06:40.767 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that was my first
introduction to the Americans.

00:06:41.834 --> 00:06:43.770 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So what did you
think of America then?

00:06:45.171 --> 00:06:47.573
- Honestly, it was more
like an alien feeling.

00:06:47.573 --> 00:06:49.241 align:start size:81% position:19%
I'm like, "Who
are these people?"

00:06:49.241 --> 00:06:52.678
And then, I mean,
thank goodness.

00:06:52.678 --> 00:06:54.580
I mean, my parents
studied in the UK

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:56.915 align:start size:97% position:3%
so I was able to speak
English even though I still,

00:06:56.915 --> 00:06:59.518
my English still depends

00:06:59.518 --> 00:07:02.421
on how much margaritas
I've had during the day.

00:07:02.421 --> 00:07:04.089
- I was gonna get
to margaritas later

00:07:04.089 --> 00:07:06.426
because the true way that
I know you're an American,

00:07:06.426 --> 00:07:09.294 align:start size:88% position:13%
you love a margarita
more than anyone I know.

00:07:09.294 --> 00:07:10.696 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And that was, yeah,
thanks to Texas.

00:07:10.696 --> 00:07:12.431
- It's technically
Mexican, but we'll...

00:07:12.431 --> 00:07:14.733 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, it's a melting pot.
- Yeah, exactly.

00:07:14.733 --> 00:07:17.069
- And so yeah, so the
first, I would say,

00:07:17.069 --> 00:07:19.171 align:start size:84% position:16%
the first interaction
with an American

00:07:19.171 --> 00:07:23.208
was an American soldier, and
they were like, very friendly.

00:07:23.208 --> 00:07:26.011 align:start size:94% position:6%
They were kind of very
curious and they would go,

00:07:26.011 --> 00:07:29.615
"We are here to like,
defeat Saddam Hussein."

00:07:29.615 --> 00:07:32.651 align:start size:94% position:6%
However, things escalated
badly very quickly

00:07:32.651 --> 00:07:35.521
so the reaction of the
American army to Iraqis

00:07:35.521 --> 00:07:38.658
became more negative as they
started getting more attacked.

00:07:38.658 --> 00:07:40.292
However, one of the benefits

00:07:40.292 --> 00:07:43.095
that came in after the
war was the internet.

00:07:43.095 --> 00:07:47.032
And I was, I mean, 2003, 2004,

00:07:47.032 --> 00:07:49.769
the internet was not as
advanced as it is today.

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:54.239 align:start size:97% position:3%
But I was able to like, I
could just explore the world

00:07:54.239 --> 00:07:57.443
through the internet and
see what America is like

00:07:57.443 --> 00:07:59.444
and what the values
of America were.

00:07:59.444 --> 00:08:01.413
So like, when all of that
civil wars was happening,

00:08:01.413 --> 00:08:03.950 align:start size:88% position:13%
I was sitting at home
reading Thomas Jefferson

00:08:04.951 --> 00:08:07.086 align:start size:88% position:13%
and "The Age of
Reason" by Thomas Paine

00:08:07.086 --> 00:08:08.888
and "Common Sense"
and stuff like that.

00:08:08.888 --> 00:08:10.555
And I was like, these are...

00:08:10.555 --> 00:08:14.059
And the story like, of America,
at least the beginning story

00:08:14.059 --> 00:08:17.196
and even like the Europe
33 Years' War, et cetera,

00:08:17.196 --> 00:08:18.897
or the origin of
the Enlightenment

00:08:18.897 --> 00:08:20.933 align:start size:97% position:3%
is what I think is the Middle
East is facing right now.

00:08:20.933 --> 00:08:23.135 align:start size:84% position:16%
So it's like, the
foundings of America

00:08:23.135 --> 00:08:26.172
and the civil wars that it
had and European civil wars

00:08:26.172 --> 00:08:28.140 align:start size:97% position:3%
when Protestants and
Catholics, stuff like that,

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:30.409
is what I witnessed,
so it's just like,

00:08:30.409 --> 00:08:33.012 align:start size:88% position:13%
we're 300 years behind.
- Right.

00:08:33.012 --> 00:08:36.215
- And my goal is to
bring those values

00:08:36.215 --> 00:08:37.716
into the region
that I came from,

00:08:37.716 --> 00:08:40.986
which I think are the
reason why thousands

00:08:40.986 --> 00:08:43.222
and hundreds of thousands
of people are prosperous

00:08:43.222 --> 00:08:44.990
are the values that
founded America,

00:08:44.990 --> 00:08:46.491 align:start size:88% position:13%
which I think are
values worth spreading.

00:08:46.491 --> 00:08:48.727
- And you quite literally are
doing that with your life.

00:08:48.727 --> 00:08:50.596 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's what Ideas
Beyond Borders does.

00:08:50.596 --> 00:08:53.599
- Exactly, and I think that
even with my disappointment,

00:08:53.599 --> 00:08:55.367
I mean, in a theoretical world,

00:08:55.367 --> 00:08:58.604
let's say if America turns
against America, let's say,

00:08:58.604 --> 00:09:00.506
I think that these values
are still worth defending.

00:09:00.506 --> 00:09:03.475 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I think is that suppressing
the values of freedom.

00:09:03.475 --> 00:09:06.178
America is itself an idea as
well as a country, and that's,

00:09:06.178 --> 00:09:08.547 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think, what is very
unique about this country

00:09:08.547 --> 00:09:10.482
is that there is, I mean,

00:09:10.482 --> 00:09:12.985
with all my love for
Canadian neighbors,

00:09:12.985 --> 00:09:16.188
but there's no Canadian idea.

00:09:16.188 --> 00:09:20.092
But America as a nation
is founded on ideals

00:09:20.092 --> 00:09:24.396
that are universal and built one

00:09:24.396 --> 00:09:27.632 align:start size:97% position:3%
of the largest freaking
economy and largest military.

00:09:27.632 --> 00:09:29.802
However, I think
that the people,

00:09:29.802 --> 00:09:32.738
those who have lived under
prosperity for a long time,

00:09:32.738 --> 00:09:34.206
and that's one of the
main contradictions

00:09:34.206 --> 00:09:36.508 align:start size:94% position:6%
is that they start
turning against the ideas

00:09:36.508 --> 00:09:38.710 align:start size:94% position:6%
that made them
prosperous at the moment.

00:09:38.710 --> 00:09:41.480 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so one of, I
would say, the ideals

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:44.783 align:start size:84% position:16%
of Ideas Beyond
Borders is making sure

00:09:44.783 --> 00:09:47.586 align:start size:97% position:3%
that the American ideals
spread, that they can continue

00:09:47.586 --> 00:09:49.421
kind of like when you
send the humans to Mars

00:09:49.421 --> 00:09:51.290 align:start size:84% position:16%
so in that way
humanity can continue

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:52.891
is to send the American
ideals all over the world

00:09:52.891 --> 00:09:56.329
so in that way, these
ideals will continue.

00:09:56.329 --> 00:09:57.863
- Does it blow your mind?

00:09:57.863 --> 00:09:59.365
I mean, when you go back,
you were just interacting

00:09:59.365 --> 00:10:01.433
not too long ago, and you
travel the world obviously.

00:10:01.433 --> 00:10:03.135 align:start size:94% position:6%
When you go to the places
where it's not okay

00:10:03.135 --> 00:10:05.037 align:start size:88% position:13%
to say what you think
and all of these things,

00:10:05.037 --> 00:10:06.272
and you're translating books

00:10:06.272 --> 00:10:08.374 align:start size:84% position:16%
so that people can
read these great texts

00:10:08.374 --> 00:10:10.576
in the countries
that aren't as open

00:10:10.576 --> 00:10:14.079 align:start size:88% position:13%
and then you compare
that to the people here,

00:10:14.079 --> 00:10:16.982
you know, who are fighting
against freedom or, you know,

00:10:16.982 --> 00:10:21.020
not really aware of how good
it is, must drive you nuts.

00:10:21.020 --> 00:10:22.154
- It does.

00:10:22.154 --> 00:10:24.857 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think it is because
that's, again,

00:10:24.857 --> 00:10:27.059 align:start size:84% position:16%
one of the
contradictions is that

00:10:27.059 --> 00:10:30.162 align:start size:88% position:13%
when you live under
freedom for a long time,

00:10:30.962 --> 00:10:32.197
you start becoming cynical.

00:10:32.197 --> 00:10:34.100
I think one of the main
things that I dislike

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:37.870 align:start size:88% position:13%
about kind of a specific
section of Americans,

00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:41.540 align:start size:97% position:3%
obviously not all, as I
said, I think most Americans

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:44.010 align:start size:81% position:19%
are apolitical
and to some extent

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:45.578
they don't know what they have

00:10:45.578 --> 00:10:48.114
and they don't necessarily
appreciate what they have,

00:10:48.114 --> 00:10:49.281
but they don't necessarily-

00:10:49.281 --> 00:10:50.783 align:start size:88% position:13%
- [Dave] It's a
function of the success.

00:10:50.783 --> 00:10:54.487
- Yeah, and I think more
like those who are cynical.

00:10:54.487 --> 00:10:56.856
Like, I remember I gave
a speech at UC Berkeley,

00:10:58.224 --> 00:11:00.092
and I was talking about like,

00:11:00.092 --> 00:11:02.027
my first experience
of a suicide bomber

00:11:02.027 --> 00:11:04.930
and how literally the ecosystem

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:06.932
that allows this
thing to happen.

00:11:06.932 --> 00:11:08.534 align:start size:81% position:19%
And one of the
students was like,

00:11:08.534 --> 00:11:10.236
"Oh, we have the
same thing here."

00:11:10.236 --> 00:11:13.105 align:start size:81% position:19%
I'm like, "Where?"
(Dave laughs)

00:11:13.105 --> 00:11:16.809
All I see is like, a shawarma
restaurant that's fucked up.

00:11:16.809 --> 00:11:18.110
I mean, the shawarma is like,

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:20.145
I don't know, made
from rabbit meat.

00:11:20.145 --> 00:11:21.980
- You're telling me a
Berkeley shawarma place

00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:23.215 align:start size:75% position:25%
isn't the best?
- No.

00:11:23.215 --> 00:11:24.583 align:start size:94% position:6%
I recommend other places.
- Yeah.

00:11:25.584 --> 00:11:27.787 align:start size:88% position:13%
- So I was like,
"Okay, seriously, like,

00:11:27.787 --> 00:11:31.056
how delusional you must
think that you think

00:11:31.056 --> 00:11:34.126
that life in America is bad
as it is in other places?"

00:11:34.126 --> 00:11:35.861
Not, I mean, some
places obviously

00:11:35.861 --> 00:11:36.929
are developed like America.

00:11:36.929 --> 00:11:38.264
Norway, I just was in Norway.

00:11:38.264 --> 00:11:39.899 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's a pretty cool country.
- Yeah.

00:11:39.899 --> 00:11:42.234 align:start size:94% position:6%
- But to say like-
- It's also a very small,

00:11:42.234 --> 00:11:44.303 align:start size:97% position:3%
largely homogenous country.
- Exactly.

00:11:44.303 --> 00:11:46.439
And when they had start
having more immigrants,

00:11:46.439 --> 00:11:49.809
even in Denmark and
other places, they
get confused, right?

00:11:49.809 --> 00:11:51.343
Because they're
very homogeneous.

00:11:51.343 --> 00:11:53.946
That's why even like systems
like democratic socialism,

00:11:53.946 --> 00:11:56.182 align:start size:84% position:16%
whatever it's
called, can operate

00:11:56.182 --> 00:11:58.017 align:start size:97% position:3%
because it's more like a
tribe that became a country.

00:11:58.017 --> 00:12:01.186 align:start size:97% position:3%
But in America it's like,
you have all of these people

00:12:01.186 --> 00:12:02.921 align:start size:84% position:16%
from different
parts of the world,

00:12:02.921 --> 00:12:05.324
most of them able to
coexist and function.

00:12:05.324 --> 00:12:07.426
I think it's pretty wonderful.

00:12:07.426 --> 00:12:11.129 align:start size:97% position:3%
And because I come from
country and a place, a region

00:12:11.129 --> 00:12:14.934 align:start size:97% position:3%
in which diversity is actually
a curse, not a blessing.

00:12:14.934 --> 00:12:16.168
- [Dave] Mm-hmm.

00:12:16.168 --> 00:12:19.738
- So many people fight
for a homogeneous place.

00:12:19.738 --> 00:12:21.474
The Kurds are fighting
for their own place.

00:12:21.474 --> 00:12:23.175
The Assyrians and the Christians

00:12:23.175 --> 00:12:24.777
want their own small territory.

00:12:24.777 --> 00:12:26.479
The Shias want their thing.

00:12:26.479 --> 00:12:27.713
And so in a way it's like,

00:12:27.713 --> 00:12:31.383
where I come from,
diversity is a curse.

00:12:31.383 --> 00:12:35.888 align:start size:97% position:3%
And while here, because
the identity of the country

00:12:35.888 --> 00:12:39.224
is built on ideals,
and unfortunately now

00:12:39.224 --> 00:12:41.626 align:start size:97% position:3%
there's a whole identitarian
identity politics world

00:12:41.626 --> 00:12:44.263
in which everything is
about race and stuff.

00:12:44.263 --> 00:12:46.732
But I think the founding of
America was about ideals,

00:12:46.732 --> 00:12:49.935 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we've seen it
functioning for 300 years,

00:12:49.935 --> 00:12:52.538 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I think it's really
worth preserving.

00:12:52.538 --> 00:12:53.806 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, I'm definitely
not giving up,

00:12:53.806 --> 00:12:56.375 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I'm trying my best
now 10 years later

00:12:56.375 --> 00:12:59.411
to be more involved in
the local conversation,

00:12:59.411 --> 00:13:01.447
even though the Middle
East is enough headache.

00:13:01.447 --> 00:13:04.383
I mean, I've had my fair share
of tequila every couple days

00:13:04.383 --> 00:13:09.455
and it's never enough,
but I think is that,

00:13:10.589 --> 00:13:12.424
I think there's a duty
of now being a citizen

00:13:12.424 --> 00:13:14.326
and being welcomed by
this wonderful country,

00:13:14.326 --> 00:13:15.594
I think it's also
part of my duty

00:13:15.594 --> 00:13:17.763 align:start size:81% position:19%
to contribute here
any way I can.

00:13:17.763 --> 00:13:19.398
- What's going on in Iraq now?

00:13:19.398 --> 00:13:20.833 align:start size:84% position:16%
I feel like we don't
talk about Iraq.

00:13:20.833 --> 00:13:22.334
Nobody knows what's going on.

00:13:22.334 --> 00:13:24.002 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, people look back
in hindsight at the war

00:13:24.002 --> 00:13:25.904 align:start size:81% position:19%
in a very...
- Mostly negative.

00:13:25.904 --> 00:13:28.273 align:start size:94% position:6%
- With very different
glasses than at the time.

00:13:28.273 --> 00:13:30.709 align:start size:97% position:3%
You actually, in retrospect,
you're happy that...

00:13:30.709 --> 00:13:31.877 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't wanna put
words in your mouth,

00:13:31.877 --> 00:13:33.913
but I think essentially happy

00:13:33.913 --> 00:13:36.549 align:start size:84% position:16%
that the Americans
liberated from Saddam,

00:13:36.549 --> 00:13:37.983
but that may not be connected

00:13:37.983 --> 00:13:39.718
to exactly what's going on now.

00:13:39.718 --> 00:13:43.755
- Yeah, I mean I support, I
wouldn't say in a direct way,

00:13:43.755 --> 00:13:46.292
like, that I support the
war, but I think is that,

00:13:46.292 --> 00:13:48.326
the options that are available.

00:13:48.326 --> 00:13:53.165 align:start position:0%
I mean, I look at Iraq from
kind of the lowest of standards,

00:13:53.165 --> 00:13:55.501 align:start size:88% position:13%
and it's mostly
because of its history.

00:13:55.501 --> 00:13:58.270
Iraq started with a king
that the British installed

00:13:58.270 --> 00:13:59.538
after the Ottoman Empire.

00:13:59.538 --> 00:14:01.607
The king was killed
by the communists,

00:14:01.607 --> 00:14:03.809
and then the socialists
killed the communists,

00:14:03.809 --> 00:14:05.544
and then Saddam Hussein
killed the socialists,

00:14:05.544 --> 00:14:07.446 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then Saddam
Hussein was attacked.

00:14:07.446 --> 00:14:08.781
That's the history of Iraq.

00:14:08.781 --> 00:14:10.982
- (laughs) In seven seconds.

00:14:10.982 --> 00:14:14.286
- So this is, so when
you're trying to like,

00:14:14.286 --> 00:14:17.189 align:start size:97% position:3%
kind of look at the ecosystem
of kind of the history,

00:14:17.189 --> 00:14:18.190
that's the history.

00:14:19.624 --> 00:14:23.462
And Saddam Hussein at his,
like the last years where,

00:14:23.462 --> 00:14:25.798
like after the first
Gulf War was his worst

00:14:25.798 --> 00:14:28.033 align:start size:88% position:13%
and was leading the
country into explosion.

00:14:29.434 --> 00:14:33.038
The alternative, I think that
if the Iraq War never happened

00:14:33.038 --> 00:14:34.240
or the US didn't intervene,

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:36.241 align:start size:84% position:16%
we would end up
exactly like Syria,

00:14:36.241 --> 00:14:39.311
which is in a weird way now
when I came back to Iraq,

00:14:39.311 --> 00:14:41.347 align:start size:94% position:6%
now there are Syria
refugees who live in Iraq.

00:14:41.347 --> 00:14:42.714
So imagine how bad Syria is

00:14:42.714 --> 00:14:45.150 align:start size:84% position:16%
that they come to Iraq
for refugee status.

00:14:45.150 --> 00:14:48.754 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I went back to
Iraq after 13 years,

00:14:48.754 --> 00:14:51.223
and I mostly went to the
Kurdistan region of Iraq,

00:14:51.223 --> 00:14:53.192 align:start size:88% position:13%
even though I was
slightly on the borders

00:14:53.192 --> 00:14:55.227
of federal Iraq and Mosul.

00:14:55.227 --> 00:15:00.299 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it is really
mind-blowing how the country,

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:03.135
at least a part of
the country became.

00:15:03.135 --> 00:15:05.604 align:start size:84% position:16%
Iraq, significantly,
has been stable,

00:15:05.604 --> 00:15:08.106
relatively, over the
past couple of years.

00:15:08.106 --> 00:15:11.309
The reasons are kind of
somehow sinister because,

00:15:11.309 --> 00:15:14.513
but it's also a reason,
I guess, of application

00:15:14.513 --> 00:15:16.982
of Milton Friedman
theories is that now

00:15:16.982 --> 00:15:19.284
the political parties of
the Sunnis and the Shias

00:15:19.284 --> 00:15:22.621
and the Kurds have enough
investments in the country.

00:15:22.621 --> 00:15:24.556
So like some of them
own shopping malls

00:15:24.556 --> 00:15:26.658
and restaurants and
all of that stuff.

00:15:26.658 --> 00:15:28.761
So pretty much all of
them now are interested

00:15:28.761 --> 00:15:30.762 align:start size:97% position:3%
in keeping the country stable.
- Mm-hmm.

00:15:30.762 --> 00:15:33.498
- So now because they
have vested interests,

00:15:33.498 --> 00:15:36.568
they no longer support suicide
bombers and stuff like that.

00:15:36.568 --> 00:15:38.771 align:start size:97% position:3%
So that's why you don't
hear about Iraq on the news

00:15:38.771 --> 00:15:41.072 align:start size:84% position:16%
because there has
not, as far as I know,

00:15:41.072 --> 00:15:43.609 align:start size:94% position:6%
there has not been a
suicide attack since 2000,

00:15:43.609 --> 00:15:45.110
like since the defeat of ISIS.

00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:48.680
And as a result, the country,
a result of that stability,

00:15:48.680 --> 00:15:51.316
there's now investments
and people coming in.

00:15:51.316 --> 00:15:56.388
And weirdly, but not weirdly,
the Kurdistan region asked us.

00:15:57.722 --> 00:15:59.858 align:start position:0%
So when I went back to Iraq
for the first time in 13 years,

00:15:59.858 --> 00:16:01.693 align:start size:88% position:13%
so I met with the
government of Kurdistan,

00:16:01.693 --> 00:16:03.495
at least a representative
of them and they were like,

00:16:03.495 --> 00:16:05.897
"We would love you
to open an office,

00:16:05.897 --> 00:16:08.467 align:start size:84% position:16%
an Ideas Beyond
Borders in this place.

00:16:08.467 --> 00:16:10.769
We are very open
to Western ideas.

00:16:10.769 --> 00:16:13.806
We would like, like,
whatever America has,

00:16:13.806 --> 00:16:15.040
we actually want some of it.

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:17.142 align:start size:84% position:16%
We want to learn
about the experiment.

00:16:17.142 --> 00:16:19.845
Like, translate more books,
work with our universities."

00:16:19.845 --> 00:16:23.114
We started like a small
kind of venture funds

00:16:23.114 --> 00:16:26.017
we call Innovation Hub
that support startups

00:16:26.017 --> 00:16:28.920 align:start size:97% position:3%
and things like that so
moving towards privatization.

00:16:28.920 --> 00:16:31.456
So in the grand
scheme of things,

00:16:31.456 --> 00:16:34.693 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think Iraq is doing much
better than I left it,

00:16:34.693 --> 00:16:36.328
which is itself a low standard,

00:16:36.328 --> 00:16:39.364 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I think is that it
definitely has the potential

00:16:39.364 --> 00:16:42.534
of becoming really a, I mean,

00:16:42.534 --> 00:16:44.569 align:start size:81% position:19%
significantly much
better country,

00:16:44.569 --> 00:16:46.572
even much better than
during Saddam Hussein.

00:16:46.572 --> 00:16:51.342 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I think when people
try to, even with,

00:16:51.342 --> 00:16:53.979
I had a debate on
"Slate Magazine,"

00:16:53.979 --> 00:16:56.048 align:start size:94% position:6%
which Christopher Hitchens
used to write for,

00:16:56.048 --> 00:16:59.051
about the Iraq war, but
"Slate" went far left.

00:16:59.051 --> 00:17:00.986 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Sure, sure.
- After Hitchens.

00:17:00.986 --> 00:17:02.821
- Like, pretty much
all the websites.

00:17:02.821 --> 00:17:04.056
- Pretty much all the websites.

00:17:04.056 --> 00:17:06.258
And the thing is
that most of the,

00:17:06.258 --> 00:17:08.360
even the conversation was,

00:17:08.360 --> 00:17:11.196 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, many of the people
who opposed the war,

00:17:11.196 --> 00:17:14.566 align:start size:97% position:3%
and some are right reasons,
some are bad reasons,

00:17:14.566 --> 00:17:16.168
but however, most
of them may try

00:17:16.168 --> 00:17:17.702
to connect to domestic affairs.

00:17:17.702 --> 00:17:20.004
Oh, Bush sucked, we don't
trust Bush and therefore,

00:17:20.004 --> 00:17:22.307 align:start position:0%
or we don't trust the Republican
Party and things like that.

00:17:22.307 --> 00:17:24.209 align:start size:84% position:16%
So eventually, I
mean, many Americans

00:17:24.209 --> 00:17:26.945
cannot even name four
provinces in Iraq.

00:17:26.945 --> 00:17:30.916
- Oh, I bet 99% can't
name probably one.

00:17:30.916 --> 00:17:32.484
- So, but at the same time

00:17:32.484 --> 00:17:33.852
have very strong
opinions about it

00:17:33.852 --> 00:17:36.522
so it it's definitely
a complicated subject.

00:17:36.522 --> 00:17:38.023 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Al Anbar Province.
How about that?

00:17:38.023 --> 00:17:39.290 align:start size:84% position:16%
Pretty good, huh?
- That's a good one.

00:17:39.290 --> 00:17:40.392
I mean, that's the
one where America

00:17:40.392 --> 00:17:41.626
did not have a good time.

00:17:41.626 --> 00:17:44.329 align:start size:84% position:16%
But yeah, with that
being said, it's like,

00:17:44.329 --> 00:17:47.632
I mean, we've seen that also
with Afghanistan is that...

00:17:47.632 --> 00:17:50.935
And that's, I would say,
is like one of the reasons

00:17:50.935 --> 00:17:53.338
why I think people like
me have to take a lead.

00:17:53.338 --> 00:17:55.707 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, I don't
believe in saviors.

00:17:55.707 --> 00:17:58.010 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think that people
from the region

00:17:58.010 --> 00:17:59.511
have to fix their own region,

00:18:00.612 --> 00:18:02.080 align:start size:88% position:13%
which is the reason I
started my organization.

00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:04.149
It's the reason why I started
support many organizations

00:18:04.149 --> 00:18:05.817
in Iraq and stuff like that.

00:18:05.817 --> 00:18:09.020
In fact, my goal of
my organization is
ceasing to exist.

00:18:09.020 --> 00:18:10.889 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah.
- Like, we don't wanna be,

00:18:10.889 --> 00:18:12.357 align:start size:84% position:16%
we don't wanna build
this bureaucracy

00:18:12.357 --> 00:18:14.759
in which you just wanna
advocate for the cause

00:18:14.759 --> 00:18:16.762
and you keep raising
money just because

00:18:17.829 --> 00:18:19.297 align:start size:88% position:13%
there is an organization
you wanna maintain.

00:18:19.297 --> 00:18:21.567 align:start size:94% position:6%
So eventually I think
the people there have to.

00:18:21.567 --> 00:18:24.803 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think America with
all its mistakes

00:18:24.803 --> 00:18:27.572
allowed the room
for that to happen.

00:18:27.572 --> 00:18:30.209
There's now free press
or freedom of speech

00:18:30.209 --> 00:18:32.978 align:start size:84% position:16%
when I know it's under
attack even here.

00:18:32.978 --> 00:18:35.013
That, I think, is like the seed

00:18:35.013 --> 00:18:36.515 align:start size:84% position:16%
of building a
functional society.

00:18:36.515 --> 00:18:39.251
It's the seed of innovation.
It's the seed of progress.

00:18:39.251 --> 00:18:41.219
And not progress towards hell

00:18:41.219 --> 00:18:43.021 align:start size:94% position:6%
in a way that progressives
in America are,

00:18:43.021 --> 00:18:45.023
but like, but actual progress.

00:18:45.023 --> 00:18:47.526 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I think that
America built that city.

00:18:47.526 --> 00:18:49.494 align:start size:84% position:16%
Even in Afghanistan,
now we're working

00:18:49.494 --> 00:18:51.062
with like, underground schools.

00:18:51.062 --> 00:18:54.299
Like, we built a network
of schools of teachers

00:18:54.299 --> 00:18:56.067
who educate girls underground

00:18:56.067 --> 00:18:57.602
from different houses
all over the place.

00:18:57.602 --> 00:18:59.605
And honestly, I'm like, "Why
the hell you do what you do?

00:18:59.605 --> 00:19:00.906
Like, what's really
the motivation?"

00:19:00.906 --> 00:19:02.941
And they're like, "We
are the first women

00:19:02.941 --> 00:19:05.710
to ever been to school
in most of our tribe.

00:19:05.710 --> 00:19:07.412
And most of the reason
why we went to school

00:19:07.412 --> 00:19:09.348
is because America
came to our country."

00:19:09.348 --> 00:19:11.517
So in that kind of 20 years,

00:19:11.517 --> 00:19:13.785 align:start size:84% position:16%
some of these women
went to school,

00:19:13.785 --> 00:19:15.187
they went to college, et cetera,

00:19:15.187 --> 00:19:17.288
and now they feel it's
their duty to make sure

00:19:17.288 --> 00:19:21.159 align:start size:97% position:3%
that the girls of tomorrow
don't end up being illiterate.

00:19:21.159 --> 00:19:23.528
So that is a success story
of the American intervention.

00:19:23.528 --> 00:19:25.997 align:start size:97% position:3%
The American withdrawals,
however, that's another story.

00:19:25.997 --> 00:19:30.702 align:start size:97% position:3%
But I think is that, I think
that we can always talk

00:19:30.702 --> 00:19:32.871
about the negatives
of the intervention,

00:19:32.871 --> 00:19:34.339
but I am seeing like, even when,

00:19:34.339 --> 00:19:36.141 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I would love to
take you to Kurdistan,

00:19:36.141 --> 00:19:39.745 align:start position:0%
and I gift for you from
Kurdistan, is that you will see

00:19:39.745 --> 00:19:41.312
that people are appreciative.

00:19:41.312 --> 00:19:42.915 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I think what
happens with the Kurds

00:19:42.915 --> 00:19:44.416
is that they took leadership.

00:19:44.416 --> 00:19:46.651
America did what it can,
but then at the same time

00:19:46.651 --> 00:19:48.620
they're like, we
want our country,

00:19:48.620 --> 00:19:50.121
our place to be developed.

00:19:50.121 --> 00:19:52.424
And they took leadership and
they made it happen, so I-

00:19:52.424 --> 00:19:54.259
- So in effect they have a...

00:19:54.259 --> 00:19:55.761 align:start size:84% position:16%
I know it's not
technically a country

00:19:55.761 --> 00:19:57.428
by international standards,

00:19:57.428 --> 00:20:00.198 align:start size:88% position:13%
but they have a country
in essence, right?

00:20:00.198 --> 00:20:01.432 align:start size:81% position:19%
Like in-
- To some extent.

00:20:01.432 --> 00:20:03.768 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, it's a
semi-autonomous region.

00:20:03.768 --> 00:20:05.036 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I mean, not
according to Turkey

00:20:05.036 --> 00:20:06.705
and not according
to Iraq, I guess.

00:20:06.705 --> 00:20:08.239
- But the politics, I mean,

00:20:08.239 --> 00:20:11.843 align:start size:97% position:3%
that needs like six bottles
of tequila, just the...

00:20:11.843 --> 00:20:15.981
But to make it very,
very simple is that,

00:20:15.981 --> 00:20:18.717
I mean, there are multiple...

00:20:18.717 --> 00:20:20.218
I know that's not the
official narrative,

00:20:20.218 --> 00:20:21.686 align:start size:88% position:13%
but there are different
types of Kurds.

00:20:21.686 --> 00:20:23.622 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Mm-hmm.
- So that's also part of it.

00:20:23.622 --> 00:20:26.558 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, even the
Kurds themselves speak
different languages.

00:20:26.558 --> 00:20:29.293
Generally speaking, the
Kurds of Iraq and Iran

00:20:29.293 --> 00:20:31.430 align:start size:97% position:3%
speak a similar language
and have a similar heritage,

00:20:31.430 --> 00:20:32.897
and the ones in Turkey and Syria

00:20:32.897 --> 00:20:35.267 align:start size:94% position:6%
have a different heritage,
different language.

00:20:35.267 --> 00:20:37.536
The ones in Turkey in particular

00:20:37.536 --> 00:20:39.605
who are supported a lot
by the Soviet Union,

00:20:39.605 --> 00:20:41.440
so there's a major
group called the PKK,

00:20:41.440 --> 00:20:45.410
which is mostly communist,
like, nut jobs in my opinion,

00:20:45.410 --> 00:20:49.481
who have extreme differences
with the ones in Iraq and Iran.

00:20:50.716 --> 00:20:52.584
So there are a lot
of kind of issues

00:20:52.584 --> 00:20:55.153
within unity for the Kurds.

00:20:55.153 --> 00:20:57.456 align:start size:84% position:16%
However, the Kurdistan
region of Iraq

00:20:57.456 --> 00:20:58.890
is kind of the most successful.

00:20:58.890 --> 00:21:00.259
It's institutionalized.

00:21:00.259 --> 00:21:02.294
They have their own parliament,

00:21:02.294 --> 00:21:03.795
and it's definitely
a success story,

00:21:03.795 --> 00:21:06.631 align:start size:97% position:3%
which I think many other Kurds
from the neighboring places

00:21:06.631 --> 00:21:10.102 align:start size:94% position:6%
can look up to as a model
that they can follow.

00:21:10.102 --> 00:21:13.405
And so if you are an Arab Iraqi,

00:21:13.405 --> 00:21:15.273
you technically need a residency

00:21:15.273 --> 00:21:16.975 align:start size:94% position:6%
when you enter the
Kurdistan region of Iraq.

00:21:16.975 --> 00:21:20.344
So kind of, it's not
a visa, but you need.

00:21:20.344 --> 00:21:22.547
If you are an Iraqi American
and then there's like, you can,

00:21:22.547 --> 00:21:25.016
even if you're Arab, you
can stay however you want.

00:21:25.016 --> 00:21:26.918 align:start size:84% position:16%
But, so it is
technically, I mean,

00:21:26.918 --> 00:21:29.921 align:start size:97% position:3%
the Kurdistan region of
Iraq, it's technically a...

00:21:29.921 --> 00:21:31.823 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, you see more
flags of Kurdistan there

00:21:31.823 --> 00:21:33.425
than you see flags of Iraq.

00:21:34.225 --> 00:21:35.627
However, there are many Arabs,

00:21:35.627 --> 00:21:37.262 align:start size:97% position:3%
and there are many investors
from the Gulf States

00:21:37.262 --> 00:21:40.465
and others building fancy
hotels and restaurants.

00:21:40.465 --> 00:21:43.568
And so the Kurds there found out

00:21:43.568 --> 00:21:45.637
that it's good to do
business with the Arabs

00:21:45.637 --> 00:21:48.774 align:start size:94% position:6%
and not be in that
constant state of turmoil.

00:21:48.774 --> 00:21:51.076 align:start position:0%
- So in some ways it's, you
sort of hinted at this earlier,

00:21:51.076 --> 00:21:52.778 align:start size:84% position:16%
you basically said it,
I mean, capitalism,

00:21:52.778 --> 00:21:54.313
the ability to just
trade with each other

00:21:54.313 --> 00:21:57.149 align:start size:97% position:3%
sort of is the first nugget
to getting to everything else.

00:21:57.149 --> 00:21:59.251 align:start size:97% position:3%
That people suddenly
realize, oh, I've got a shop.

00:21:59.251 --> 00:22:00.752 align:start size:94% position:6%
I've got employees,
there's people around me.

00:22:00.752 --> 00:22:02.888 align:start size:88% position:13%
Like, there's something
worth protecting.

00:22:02.888 --> 00:22:04.956
- 100%, I'm 100% a believer

00:22:04.956 --> 00:22:08.259
in the kind of the
how connectedness.

00:22:08.259 --> 00:22:10.929 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean even now with
Saudi Arabia, I mean, yes,

00:22:10.929 --> 00:22:12.831
we can talk a lot about MBS,

00:22:12.831 --> 00:22:16.133
but if you look at it from
the core, like Saudi Arabia,

00:22:16.133 --> 00:22:19.337 align:start size:97% position:3%
because of the fact oil, they
wanna diversify from oil.

00:22:19.337 --> 00:22:22.273
Before they have oil, they
can say whatever they want.

00:22:22.273 --> 00:22:24.843 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, it's our oil. You
don't like us, go to hell.

00:22:24.843 --> 00:22:27.278
Now they are, they want to
open up for investments.

00:22:27.278 --> 00:22:29.414 align:start size:97% position:3%
To open up for investments,
you need to liberalize

00:22:29.414 --> 00:22:33.184
and you need to be open to
capitalism and the fact that

00:22:33.184 --> 00:22:36.554
when you're gonna bring in
people from Florida and LA

00:22:36.554 --> 00:22:40.859 align:start size:97% position:3%
and everywhere, they would
like to bring their girlfriend

00:22:40.859 --> 00:22:43.561 align:start size:94% position:6%
or their wife or they'd
like to bring the husband.

00:22:43.561 --> 00:22:47.431 align:start size:94% position:6%
So as a result, they have
to, so like in a way,

00:22:47.431 --> 00:22:50.736 align:start position:0%
the economic freedom or the
incentive to create investments

00:22:50.736 --> 00:22:52.137
is opening up Saudi Arabia,

00:22:52.137 --> 00:22:56.241 align:start size:97% position:3%
which is the most conservative
place in the region.

00:22:56.241 --> 00:23:01.146
So I think it's that, that's
why I'm wearing this dollar.

00:23:02.213 --> 00:23:03.215 align:start size:84% position:16%
Even though it's
actually the pencil,

00:23:04.449 --> 00:23:06.817
which talks about how
like the pencil itself

00:23:06.817 --> 00:23:09.654 align:start position:0%
is made from different
elements and it needed the work

00:23:09.654 --> 00:23:11.656 align:start size:88% position:13%
of multiple people from
different countries

00:23:11.656 --> 00:23:13.525
to work together
to make the pencil.

00:23:13.525 --> 00:23:16.962
And that's, I think,
is essential piece

00:23:16.962 --> 00:23:18.530
if we want to move the region.

00:23:18.530 --> 00:23:20.932 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that's a great thing about
investments and capitalism.

00:23:20.932 --> 00:23:22.867
It reduces dependency.

00:23:22.867 --> 00:23:25.403 align:start size:84% position:16%
Most of these regions,
and because of

00:23:25.403 --> 00:23:28.340
these shitty international
development organizations

00:23:28.340 --> 00:23:30.041
like the UN and all of that,

00:23:30.041 --> 00:23:32.043
what they do is that
they create dependency.

00:23:32.043 --> 00:23:34.645 align:start size:88% position:13%
They give all, here's
these, here's some food,

00:23:34.645 --> 00:23:38.049
here's some stuff, but
then they create a system

00:23:38.049 --> 00:23:40.652 align:start position:0%
in which you have to
constantly give money to the UN

00:23:40.652 --> 00:23:41.886
for people not to starve.

00:23:41.886 --> 00:23:43.354
- It's just incredible
'cause it's what we have

00:23:43.354 --> 00:23:45.790 align:start size:97% position:3%
with all of our welfare state,
and then we've exported it

00:23:45.790 --> 00:23:48.059 align:start size:88% position:13%
into these giant
globalist organizations.

00:23:48.059 --> 00:23:49.761
- With, in some
cases, bad ideas.

00:23:49.761 --> 00:23:51.997 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, I think the study,
yeah, in Iraq about,

00:23:51.997 --> 00:23:54.833
they built a school for like,

00:23:54.833 --> 00:23:56.468
people who identify as girls

00:23:56.468 --> 00:23:57.669
or something like
that and people-

00:23:57.669 --> 00:23:59.738 align:start size:75% position:25%
- In Iraq?
- Yeah. (laughs)

00:23:59.738 --> 00:24:01.840
And people have no idea
how to translate that

00:24:01.840 --> 00:24:03.074 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the first place.
- Oh my God.

00:24:03.074 --> 00:24:05.477 align:start size:81% position:19%
- So there is also
a backlash now,

00:24:05.477 --> 00:24:07.478 align:start size:84% position:16%
a significant
backlash, I would say,

00:24:07.478 --> 00:24:09.814
against some of the
ideologies that come

00:24:09.814 --> 00:24:11.349
with these international
development organizations

00:24:11.349 --> 00:24:15.520 align:start size:84% position:16%
that they kind of give
you a bag of food,

00:24:15.520 --> 00:24:17.756
but also comes with
the whole baggage of-

00:24:17.756 --> 00:24:19.624
- But you have to dress
your son up like a woman.

00:24:19.624 --> 00:24:22.994
- Yes, I mean, that will go
far away in Anbar province.

00:24:22.994 --> 00:24:24.262
- Yeah.

00:24:24.262 --> 00:24:26.764
- (laughs) So there is
definitely a backlash.

00:24:26.764 --> 00:24:29.567
And we try to, I mean, as
an organization that's new

00:24:29.567 --> 00:24:32.237
and we try to work,
we have our ideals,

00:24:32.237 --> 00:24:35.139 align:start size:94% position:6%
but we try to work
within the region itself.

00:24:35.139 --> 00:24:38.843 align:start position:0%
And even now, I mean, the
conversation is very difficult,

00:24:38.843 --> 00:24:40.912 align:start size:84% position:16%
like, to tell people
to Westernize.

00:24:40.912 --> 00:24:42.514
But even if you tell
them to Westernize,

00:24:42.514 --> 00:24:45.883
what version of the West
they should Westernize to.

00:24:45.883 --> 00:24:49.654
So the thing is that the
main thing is to get people

00:24:49.654 --> 00:24:52.523 align:start size:84% position:16%
into these ideals
and let them make up.

00:24:52.523 --> 00:24:56.560 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I don't think, like,
the ultimate goal of freedom

00:24:56.560 --> 00:24:58.596 align:start size:97% position:3%
when people get to know
about classical liberalism,

00:24:58.596 --> 00:25:01.866 align:start size:97% position:3%
they end up subscribing to the
woke totalitarian ideology.

00:25:01.866 --> 00:25:04.870
I think is that they
become self-evident

00:25:04.870 --> 00:25:06.871 align:start size:88% position:13%
and they were like,
"We want to keep that."

00:25:06.871 --> 00:25:07.973 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So that's actually
a great segue

00:25:07.973 --> 00:25:09.474
to where I wanted to go next,

00:25:09.474 --> 00:25:12.511
which is that when I first met
you, there was a really huge

00:25:12.511 --> 00:25:15.680 align:start size:88% position:13%
sort of secular humanist
movement in America.

00:25:15.680 --> 00:25:17.115 align:start size:81% position:19%
- [Faisal] Oh,
the good old days.

00:25:17.115 --> 00:25:18.750 align:start size:97% position:3%
- You and I went to the
Reason Rally in D.C. together,

00:25:18.750 --> 00:25:20.451 align:start size:81% position:19%
and I spoke at
the National Mall.

00:25:20.451 --> 00:25:22.587
And this is at the sort
of height of Sam Harris,

00:25:22.587 --> 00:25:26.491 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you mentioned Hitchens
before and Dawkins.

00:25:26.491 --> 00:25:28.226
And there was this
feeling of like,

00:25:28.226 --> 00:25:31.229
you could escape all
of the old of religion

00:25:31.229 --> 00:25:34.131
and create this sort of
perfect secular thing,

00:25:34.131 --> 00:25:37.101
except it's pretty
much fallen apart.

00:25:37.101 --> 00:25:39.871 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I suspect that most
of your support these days

00:25:39.871 --> 00:25:42.640
is somewhat connected
to people of faith

00:25:42.640 --> 00:25:44.942 align:start size:84% position:16%
more than purely
secular organizations.

00:25:44.942 --> 00:25:46.444
Is that fair to say?

00:25:46.444 --> 00:25:49.747
- Both, I mean, I would say
where most of our support

00:25:49.747 --> 00:25:52.117 align:start size:88% position:13%
comes up from the
classical liberal world.

00:25:53.251 --> 00:25:55.486
That would be like Atlas
Network and kind of the,

00:25:55.486 --> 00:25:57.022
and people who support

00:25:57.022 --> 00:26:00.091 align:start size:88% position:13%
like the libertarian
classical liberal space.

00:26:00.091 --> 00:26:01.993 align:start size:84% position:16%
I would say very
little of our support

00:26:01.993 --> 00:26:03.361
comes from that humanist world.

00:26:03.361 --> 00:26:05.530 align:start size:84% position:16%
In fact, that secular
humanist world

00:26:05.530 --> 00:26:07.431 align:start size:84% position:16%
generally teared
against us, is that-

00:26:07.431 --> 00:26:08.899 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Right.
- They were like,

00:26:08.899 --> 00:26:12.036 align:start size:97% position:3%
oh, you're trying to spread
the ideas of capitalism

00:26:12.036 --> 00:26:14.205
that led to slavery
and shit like that.

00:26:14.205 --> 00:26:16.708
- Right, so the people that
you thought were your...

00:26:16.708 --> 00:26:18.142
that's what I was trying get to.

00:26:18.142 --> 00:26:19.644 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.
- People that you thought

00:26:19.644 --> 00:26:21.279
were your greatest allies,
probably, when you got here

00:26:21.279 --> 00:26:23.048
and me as a liberal back then.

00:26:23.048 --> 00:26:24.482
- Yeah, yeah, yeah. They turned-

00:26:24.482 --> 00:26:26.518 align:start position:0%
- They were the ones that ended
up turning on us the most.

00:26:26.518 --> 00:26:28.753 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And they were
supporting, in fact,

00:26:28.753 --> 00:26:30.488 align:start size:84% position:16%
the groups that were
going against us.

00:26:30.488 --> 00:26:33.524 align:start size:84% position:16%
So because of
cultural relativism.

00:26:33.524 --> 00:26:35.693
That was one of, I would
say the, central tenets

00:26:35.693 --> 00:26:40.765
of these groups that
were associated with
is that they were,

00:26:42.099 --> 00:26:44.135
they hated the values that
made this country prosperous.

00:26:44.135 --> 00:26:47.205
So in a way is that they,
most of their criticism

00:26:47.205 --> 00:26:48.973 align:start size:81% position:19%
is of America than
other cultures.

00:26:48.973 --> 00:26:51.376 align:start size:97% position:3%
So in the moment you say,
"Oh, there is issues happening

00:26:51.376 --> 00:26:53.244
in the Islamic world
or in the Middle East"

00:26:53.244 --> 00:26:56.014
and things like that, they
always try to drive it back.

00:26:56.014 --> 00:26:57.816 align:start size:81% position:19%
But do you know
America is racist?

00:26:57.816 --> 00:27:00.084
And do you know that like if-

00:27:00.084 --> 00:27:02.887 align:start size:97% position:3%
- You were usually thinking
about that in Iraq.

00:27:02.887 --> 00:27:04.956 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, yeah.
- In '97, weren't you?

00:27:04.956 --> 00:27:06.457
Come on.
- Exactly.

00:27:06.457 --> 00:27:08.159 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's like when I was young,
when I was in '97, I was like,

00:27:08.159 --> 00:27:09.860 align:start size:84% position:16%
"Oh shit, maybe I
should stay in Iraq.

00:27:09.860 --> 00:27:11.295 align:start size:97% position:3%
Why would I move to New York?"
- Right.

00:27:11.295 --> 00:27:16.368 align:start size:97% position:3%
- And so that is, generally
these kind of groups,

00:27:17.035 --> 00:27:19.470
I think that the...

00:27:19.470 --> 00:27:21.840
I mean, there are secular
groups even within like,

00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:23.942
the Ayn Rand crowd,
the objectivists

00:27:23.942 --> 00:27:25.376
that were not originally.

00:27:25.376 --> 00:27:27.612 align:start size:94% position:6%
So like, the secular
world is kind of divided.

00:27:27.612 --> 00:27:32.049 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's between those who, mostly
on the left, unfortunately.

00:27:32.049 --> 00:27:34.085 align:start position:0%
And there are those who are
like, kind of the objectivists,

00:27:34.085 --> 00:27:35.820
and they are clashing.

00:27:35.820 --> 00:27:37.555
That's why you don't
see them being invited

00:27:37.555 --> 00:27:41.425
to these largely kind
of humanist places.

00:27:41.425 --> 00:27:46.498 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think that it's
generally terrible for...

00:27:47.832 --> 00:27:50.968
'Cause I see a separation
between the importance

00:27:50.968 --> 00:27:54.004
of separating religion
from the state, which is,

00:27:54.004 --> 00:27:57.041
'cause I've been the victim
when that didn't happen,

00:27:57.041 --> 00:27:59.143
in which the Sunnis
of their own version

00:27:59.143 --> 00:28:00.611
want to make it the case

00:28:00.611 --> 00:28:02.747 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the Shias want to
make the case, et cetera.

00:28:04.081 --> 00:28:07.985 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think that kind of, these
values can be separated

00:28:07.985 --> 00:28:10.622
from concepts like
the welfare state.

